<Docs slug="terminals">
  <h1>Terminals</h1>
  <p class="lead">The sidebar lists every project's tasks and terminals. Pick one and it fills the window.</p>

  <h2>Sidebar</h2>
  <p>
    Under <strong>Projects</strong>, each project lists its tasks in board order. A task with several terminals
    shows each one under it. A task in progress or in review with no terminal open is listed too, marked with a
    moon until you open one. Shells that belong to no task come after the tasks. An agent that finished while
    you were looking elsewhere gets a blue dot until you open it, here and everywhere else it is listed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Click a project to go back to the board or terminals you last had open there, even after a restart. Click
    its arrow to fold it away. Dots next to the name then show which agents are still working and which have
    finished unseen. On the
    board, <strong>Terminals</strong> stacks every terminal open in the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A project's row has buttons for its board and a new task. Its <strong>…</strong> menu, also on right-click,
    opens a new terminal, on your computer or in one of the project's sandboxes. The same menu opens the
    project's pull requests, issues, analysis and settings. Right-click a task for the same menu the board gives it. Switch to
    <strong>Tags</strong> to list terminals under their tags instead, with untagged ones under
    <strong>No tag</strong>. Hide the sidebar with the button at its top. Hover the left edge to bring it
    back.
  </p>

  <h2>Card stack</h2>
  <p>
    Each terminal runs your default shell in the task's worktree. A task's terminals stack, with the active one in
    front and the rest peeking out behind it. Click a background card to bring it forward. Cards stay in the
    order you last used them, so the one you just left is directly behind the one in front. A shell is a card
    of its own. When an agent runs in a card, its status (thinking or idle) shows on the card even when it is
    behind others. See <a href="/docs/harnesses/">Supported agents</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>⌘K</code> / <code>Ctrl+K</code></td><td>Open the <a href="/docs/palette/">command palette</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘I</code> / <code>Ctrl+I</code></td><td>New terminal in the project on screen</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘W</code> / <code>Ctrl+W</code></td><td>Close terminal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘1</code>-<code>⌘9</code> / <code>Ctrl+1</code>-<code>Ctrl+9</code></td><td>Select a card in the stack by position</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘T</code> / <code>Ctrl+T</code></td><td>Open the project's board</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘N</code> / <code>Ctrl+N</code></td><td>New task</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘P</code> / <code>Ctrl+P</code></td><td>Open the runner <a href="/docs/panels/">panel</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>⌘D</code> / <code>Ctrl+D</code></td><td>Toggle the <a href="/docs/diff/">diff panel</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Tag terminals</h2>
  <p>
    Tag a terminal from the tag button on its card. A terminal can carry several tags, and two terminals of one task
    can carry different ones. Tags survive a restart. The sidebar's <strong>Tags</strong> view groups what's
    running by them, listing a terminal under each of its tags.
  </p>
  <p>Manage tags from the shell or an agent with <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit tag</code></a>.</p>

  <h2 id="resume">Resume a session</h2>
  <img
    class="docs-screenshot"
    src="/assets/screenshots/resume.png"
    alt="Resume banner listing the previous session's terminals"
    loading="lazy"
  />
  <p>With nothing open, the window offers to resume your last session and lists recent tasks.</p>
  <p>
    Quitting saves your open terminals: their project, task, worktree, sandbox, order, tags, and panels, with
    split sizes and the diff panel. On the next launch, <strong>Resume last session</strong> reopens each one in
    its worktree with its panels. <strong>Dismiss</strong> clears it. Terminals whose project or task is gone, or
    whose task is Done, are skipped.
  </p>
  <p>
    Restored terminals come back as plain shells, so resuming never starts an agent on its own. To pick up an
    agent's conversation, use its own resume, such as a <code>continue</code> hook running
    <code>claude -c</code>.
  </p>
</Docs>
